## How to *Extend* Your Screen (The Wayland Problem)

I must apologize for a mistake in my previous instructions. **There is no GNOME extension for creating virtual monitors on Wayland.** I provided incorrect information regarding "Virtual Monitors by AlynxZhou". 

Because you are using **Wayland** (the modern Linux display server), it has very strict security rules that prevent software like Weylus from creating "fake" extended monitors. Currently, Weylus on Wayland can only **mirror** an *existing* display.

If you absolutely must have an **extended** display (a second screen) with full Apple Pencil support, here are your real options:

### Option 1: Use X11 instead of Wayland (Recommended)
Zorin OS has X11 installed by default. X11 allows software to easily create virtual displays.
1. Save your work and **Log Out** of Zorin OS.
2. Click on your username on the login screen.
3. Click the **Gear icon** (⚙️) in the bottom right corner of the screen.
4. Select **"Zorin Desktop on Xorg"** (instead of Wayland).
5. Type your password and log in.
6. Now, Weylus can use native `xrandr` tools to create an extended display seamlessly!

### Option 2: The Hardware Dummy Plug ($5)
If you want to stay on Wayland, the most reliable and lag-free way to create an extended monitor is to buy a cheap "HDMI Dummy Plug" (or Headless Ghost) from Amazon. 
1. Plug it into your laptop's HDMI port.
2. Your laptop will think a second monitor is connected.
3. Open Weylus and select that second monitor to stream it to your iPad!

### Option 3: GNOME Remote Desktop Trick (Clunky)
GNOME has a built-in virtual monitor feature, but it only activates when an RDP client connects.
1. Run this in your terminal: `gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.remote-desktop.rdp screen-share-mode extend`
2. Enable "Remote Desktop" in Zorin Settings.
3. Connect from your iPad using the **Microsoft Remote Desktop** app. This spawns the virtual monitor.
4. Then, open **Weylus** on your laptop and select that new virtual monitor to stream it to Safari, while leaving the RDP app running in the background on your iPad.

> [!TIP]
> Whichever option you choose, make sure to check **"Enable Uinput"** in the Weylus interface to enable Apple Pencil pressure mapping!
